  "body": "Conjecture vs rule/code: the conjecture does not misread the rule. The rule says: \"The object scored is the last one inside a code fence when any fence holds one, else the last top-level object in the text...\" For Reply A the fence holds the object {\"a\": 1}, so the rule would score {\"a\": 1}. The code, however, only treats the entire fenced span as a candidate. In Reply A the fenced span is '{\"a\": 1}\\nsome prose', which does not parse as a dict, so the fenced pool is skipped and the top-level fallback returns {\"b\": 2}. Thus the rule as written and the code part company; the conjecture correctly reports the code's actual behavior and the divergence.\n\nNext conjecture should try a fenced span that is exactly one JSON object with no prose, e.g. ```json\\n{\"a\":1}\\n```\\n{\"b\":2}, and predict the code returns {\"a\":1}. That would test whether the fenced pool works when the whole span parses, isolating the extra-prose failure. Avoid repeating the current input's prose inside the fence, because that guarantees the fenced candidate is unparseable and cannot exercise the 'last one inside a code fence' clause.\n\nReading c290ab046e92da73: it claims to render the conjecture's words 'The check gives the same answer for both: it returns {\"b\": 2} for Reply A and {\"b\": 2} for Reply B.' It does not render them. The reading has only Reply A as input and executes only that one input; it never runs Reply B, so it cannot verify 'same answer for both.' Its commitment 'expect': 'unchanged' is not stated by the conjecture, which states the output is {\"b\":2}. The reading also labels the expected output as 'rewritten': {\"b\":2}, but the conjecture did not propose rewriting the input to that object. The defect is in the reading, not the conjecture: the conjecture explicitly provides two reply texts and a falsification condition about different objects, while the reading collapses them into one input and an unstated expectation.",
